# Clipboard bridge — reverse-forward the Mac clipboard listener into remote
# hosts so nvim there can paste it into a register (`"+p`). Pulled in by
# ~/.ssh/config via `Include ~/.ssh/config.d/*`, like personal.config.
#
# The remote endpoint is a *unix socket* (created by sshd, owned by the login
# user, 0600) rather than a TCP port, so nothing else on a shared remote host
# can read the clipboard. It forwards to the Mac's loopback listener on TCP 2489
# (the clipboard-bridge launchd service: socat EXEC'ing pbpaste per connection).
# nvim reads it with `nc -U "$HOME/.clipboard-bridge.sock"` — the socket path
# is $HOME-relative on purpose so the same nvim code works on both hosts.
#
# `%r` expands to the *effective* remote login user (man ssh_config: RemoteForward
# accepts %-tokens in the socket path), so we don't hardcode the account — handy
# when a remote Host block overrides User. Only the home *prefix* differs by OS,
# so it's per host. nvim reads "$HOME/.clipboard-bridge.sock", which matches
# either path.
#
# StreamLocalBindUnlink clears a stale socket left by an unclean master exit;
# without it the forward fails with "remote port forwarding failed" on the
# next connect. RemoteForward is additive, so these blocks layer cleanly on
# top of any other Host definition for the same alias.
#
# Host-specific blocks for non-public/work remotes are intentionally NOT kept
# here. They live in a loose, unmanaged ~/.ssh/config.d/ file that chezmoi
# never tracks, per the local-only SSH policy documented in config.example.

Host thiago-mac-mini.local
  RemoteForward /Users/%r/.clipboard-bridge.sock 127.0.0.1:2489
  StreamLocalBindUnlink yes
